>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `ExclX`

``` hol4
simpLib.ExclX : string -> thm
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Excludes named simplifier components for one invocation.

`ExclX name` is a directive in the theorem list of a simplification
conversion or tactic. Unlike literal [`simpLib.Excl`](#simpLib.Excl), it
resolves an exclusion target and supports explicit namespaces:

- `rule:name` excludes matching rewrites, conversions and named decision
  procedures.
  Rewrite names accept `Thy$Name` as well as dotted qualification, and
  the theory need not be an ancestor of the current theory. Numeric
  dotted suffixes retain their usual meaning; names with too many dots
  are still invalid. Decision procedures match the exact name string
  after `rule:`.
- `looper:name` excludes the ordinary looper named `name`.
- `solver:name` excludes the named solver from the safe and unsafe lists.
- `split:name` excludes the matching `split name` and `split_asm name`
  displays, including rules registered with the `split` attribute.
- `case:ty` excludes the datatype splits for `ty`, which may be an
  unqualified type operator or a kernel-qualified `Thy$Tyop`.

A bare name first goes through the same strict dots-only parser as
`Excl` and `-*`, including its ancestry check. A matching rewrite or
named decision procedure takes precedence over every strategy target.
Only if neither matches does lookup fall back to ordinary loopers,
solvers, split displays and case displays. Matching more than one of
these strategy kinds is an ambiguity error; use a namespace to select
the intended kind. Bare names cannot bypass strict parsing: use
`ExclX "case:bool"`, not `ExclX "split.case bool"`, whose dot invokes
the strict parser.

Every unmatched `ExclX` name is an error, including an unmatched bare
name. This differs from the silent unmatched deletion of `Excl` and `-*`.
Constructing a reusable conversion or tactic need not process the directive
until application. The supplied simpset is unchanged; exclusions affect only
the invocation. Constructing or processing this marker does not enable the
persistent `split` attribute. Local split rules and TypeBase targets remain
available without registration.

### History and repetition

`ss && [ExclX name]` records the exclusion in the resulting simpset's
history. Rule exclusions retain their resolved pattern, even for bare
names; unlike `Excl`, replay does not reparse them in the current theory.
Rule, ordinary looper and solver deletions act at that point in history:
a later addition can reinstall the target.

Split/case exclusions instead record suppression of the resolved displays,
including across fragment removal/replay and `Context.restore`. A later
local addition with the same excluded display remains suppressed. This is
local simpset state, not a persistent theorem-set REMOVE; use
`splitLib.exclude_split` for the latter.

Replaying suppression is idempotent, but a newly processed `ExclX` must
still resolve a target. Repeating a rule, ordinary looper or solver deletion
usually fails once its target is gone. Repeating a split/case exclusion
works while its ambient persistent/TypeBase target is still present; a
removed local-only split no longer matches.

`Once (ExclX name)` and `Ntimes (ExclX name) n` are still invocation-wide
controls, not bounded deletions: the bound does not count exclusions or
rewrite uses. Requirement wrappers (`Req0`, `ReqD`) retain their usual
precedence and checks; wrapping an exclusion does not bypass them.

### Failure

The constructor never fails: it stores the string without parsing or
looking up a target. Processing the directive can fail for an invalid
name, an ambiguous strategy target or any unmatched name.

### Example

``` hol4
>> QCONV (SIMP_CONV bool_ss [simpLib.ExclX "rule:bool$AND_CLAUSES"])
     “T ∧ p”;
>> QCONV (SIMP_CONV bool_ss
     [Once (simpLib.ExclX "rule:bool$AND_CLAUSES")]) “T ∧ p”;
```

